{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"On node1, only root and opsadmin can schedule cron jobs.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Cronie was configured with an exact root/opsadmin allow-list owned by root with mode 0600. Root retained access, opsadmin successfully installed and executed a scheduled job, and non-allowlisted alpine was denied. The policy, account, service, and denial behavior remained intact after reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-378410fe7cca4953","cmd-bc6bba05c14844ab","cmd-ca3cd2e3423f4ccc","cmd-cdab003ac4024f6a","cmd-942dbae489b146ed","cmd-2427ad6735e143b7"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Keep the installed operating-system release and kernel.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Alpine Linux 3.23.3 and kernel 6.18.7-0-lts were directly observed before execution and after completion, including across the reboot.","evidence_ids":["global:before_prepare:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:platform","cmd-ca3cd2e3423f4ccc","cmd-cdab003ac4024f6a"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Use software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Cronie 1.7.2-r0 and its OpenRC integration were obtained from the configured Alpine v3.23 repository, installed successfully, and demonstrated functional scheduling and boot-time recovery on the retained platform.","evidence_ids":["cmd-ebe5eac1afa64aee","cmd-dc3945ba37a94ca9","cmd-1e6412bb55324136","cmd-65a97e57700141bf","cmd-942dbae489b146ed"]}],"confidence":0.98,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.94,"summary":"All substantive mutations—installing Cronie, creating opsadmin and the allow-list, switching the OpenRC service, bounded crontab tests, and rebooting—had clear task or mandatory restart-evidence roles. Test crontabs, marker files, backups, and temporary cache directories were reverted, with clean final proof. No unnecessary troubleshooting mutation was identified. Two executor-created diagnostic files remained: /tmp/apk-update.log from cmd-ebe5eac1afa64aee and /tmp/benchmark-reboot.log from cmd-50d0b7f529754f9d. This is minor abandoned residue. Package-health output was unchanged and no new listening service exposure appeared.","evidence_ids":["cmd-ebe5eac1afa64aee","cmd-50d0b7f529754f9d","cmd-e9203f133f6f4ad7","cmd-2427ad6735e143b7","global:before_prepare:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:before_prepare:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied. The mandatory node1 restart protocol is strong: it captures a healthy pre-restart baseline, acknowledgement, connection disruption, a changed boot identity independently corroborated by lifecycle snapshots, and recovered service, policy, account, scheduling behavior, OS, and kernel state. Only minor temporary-log residue detracts from hygiene.","limitations":["Behavioral denial was sampled with the non-allowlisted alpine account rather than every system account; the exact two-entry /etc/cron.allow provides coherent supporting evidence for the broader exclusion.","The lifecycle failed-service probe used systemd and was unsupported on Alpine; direct OpenRC status, process, runlevel, and post-reboot functional evidence cover the relevant cron service.","The standalone topology has no cluster state to validate during the provider-wide restart protocol."]}
